WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.780 --> 00:00:06.370
A couple years ago I had the opportunity to travel to Beirut Lebanon to give a talk.

00:00:06.370 --> 00:00:11.809
It was an amazing experience. I fell in love with the city, the culture, the heritage and

00:00:11.809 --> 00:00:13.370
most of all the people.

00:00:13.370 --> 00:00:18.820
In the course of my initial visit I was introduced
to a remarkable woman named May El Khalil.

00:00:18.820 --> 00:00:24.400
She's the founder of the Beirut Marathon,
which has grown into the largest running event

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:26.070
in the entire Middle East.

00:00:26.070 --> 00:00:32.559
In 2015 May invited me run the marathon and
I jumped on the chance to return to Beirut.

00:00:32.559 --> 00:00:38.230
Burrito bowls. Water. I’m all set to board.

00:00:38.230 --> 00:00:42.480
My impression of the Middle East had been
historically informed by mainstream media

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:48.739
as this very unsafe, dangerous corner of the
world where tensions run high and anything

00:00:48.739 --> 00:00:51.210
can happen anywhere at anytime.

00:00:56.620 --> 00:01:01.889
What I found instead was an incredibly beautiful,
dynamic city overflowing with history and

00:01:01.889 --> 00:01:08.520
teeming with warm and sophisticated people
with tremendous hope and resolve for a better

00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:09.460
Lebanon.

00:01:09.460 --> 00:01:15.200
Sure – the city is far more threadbare,
worn, war-torn and strife-riddled than its

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:21.159
pre civil war, Paris Of The Middle East heyday
in the mid-1960’s. But it remains a truly

00:01:21.159 --> 00:01:23.710
incredible place.

00:01:23.710 --> 00:01:28.170
Pretty jet lagged. But I’m going to go out
for a run.

00:01:33.950 --> 00:01:34.450
Run done.

00:01:34.450 --> 00:02:01.299
So we’re in a cab. Where are we going? This
is Isabelle. Hi Isabelle. Are you running

00:02:01.299 --> 00:02:12.040
the marathon on Sunday? Your first time? Me
too! Give me five.

00:02:12.040 --> 00:02:16.090
So there’s a lot of people who have been
asking me, “why are you going to Beirut

00:02:16.090 --> 00:02:21.310
to run a marathon? You can run a marathon
anywhere. Why Beirut?”

00:02:21.310 --> 00:02:27.440
The reason I am here is because I believe
running can change the world. I believe that

00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:33.790
running can be a very powerful instrument
of peace – particularly in a place like

00:02:33.790 --> 00:02:40.379
Lebanon where strife – both political and
religious – runs deep and has been going

00:02:40.379 --> 00:02:41.650
on for a very long time.

00:02:41.650 --> 00:02:45.750
May El Khalil: Hello Rich. It is wonderful
to have you with us.

00:02:45.750 --> 00:02:52.310
May El Khalil TED Talk: “For one day a year,
we truly stand united. And that’s when the

00:02:52.310 --> 00:02:54.530
marathon takes place.”

00:02:54.530 --> 00:02:59.450
Rich Roll: An avid runner herself, May was
inspired to start the marathon after a near-fatal

00:02:59.450 --> 00:03:04.730
accident that left her in a coma. Countless
surgeries, hospitalized for two years, she

00:03:04.730 --> 00:03:07.019
would never run again.

00:03:07.019 --> 00:03:10.879
But the resolve from this personal struggle
motivated her to give the gift of running

00:03:10.879 --> 00:03:14.170
to a city and a country itself struggling
to survive.

00:03:14.170 --> 00:03:20.730
May El Khalil: I needed to dream big, I needed
something to take me out of my pain. An objective

00:03:20.730 --> 00:03:21.989
to look forward to.

00:03:21.989 --> 00:03:29.459
Rich Roll: Sunni, Shia, Armenian, Palestinian,
Egyptian, Syrian, Jordanian, American –30,000

00:03:29.459 --> 00:03:35.049
runners from all walks of opposing political,
cultural and religious perspectives congregate

00:03:35.049 --> 00:03:42.659
annually, set aside their differences and
just run – a literal moving peace procession.

00:03:42.659 --> 00:04:00.540
May El Khalil: And when the gunfire went off,
this time it was a signal to run in harmony.

00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:09.849
It is a platform for hope and cooperation
in an ever-fragile and unstable part of the

00:04:09.849 --> 00:04:22.800
world. From Boston to Beirut, we stand as
one. After all, peace making is not a sprint

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:29.220
– it is more of a marathon.

00:04:29.220 --> 00:04:35.050
We begin in Lebanon, where the Islamic State
has claimed responsibility for one of the

00:04:35.050 --> 00:04:37.620
worst attacks to hit Beirut in years.

00:04:37.620 --> 00:04:40.190
Panic and confusion in Beirut.

00:04:40.190 --> 00:04:55.530
As of last count has killed at least 43 people,
wounded over 200 more…

00:04:55.530 --> 00:05:00.520
Just 2 days after the marathon, violence returned
to Beirut when ISIS attacked a neighborhood

00:05:00.520 --> 00:05:07.720
just down the street from my hotel. A neighborhood
I had walked through just a few nights prior.

00:05:07.720 --> 00:05:09.360
I was devastated.

00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:14.020
Beirut was no longer this distant, foreign,
unrelatable place that just pops up on CNN

00:05:14.020 --> 00:05:21.490
from time to time. It was real. And Its people
were my friends. That’s what travel does.

00:05:21.490 --> 00:05:24.050
It breeds empathy.

00:05:24.050 --> 00:05:29.410
But when I reached out to my new friends,
I was met not with depair, but rather an even

00:05:29.410 --> 00:05:31.850
firmer resolve to forge unity

00:05:31.850 --> 00:05:38.759
To echo May El Khalil, making peace truly
is a marathon.

00:05:38.759 --> 00:05:41.190
But it’s a marathon worth running.

